# EngineX AI — configuration. Copy to .env.local and fill in what you have.
# (.env* is gitignored apart from this template, so secrets stay local.)
#
# REQUIRED: DATABASE_URL. Everything else is optional — with no other keys set,
# briefs and outreach drafts fall back to a deterministic template engine, and
# "Send email" runs in dry-run mode. Webhook integrations (Slack, Teams, Make,
# Zapier) need no env vars — paste the webhook URL on the /integrations page.
#
# Deploying? A production instance additionally needs SINGLE_TENANT_SLUG (which
# workspace it serves), and should set RESET_SECRET and INGEST_SECRET. GET
# /api/health reports whether the database answers, whether a workspace can be
# resolved, and which adapters are live. The README's "Deploying this app" has
# the walkthrough.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database — Postgres. Required: the app stores every tenant's workspace here.
#
# Any Postgres works: a local one, Neon, Render, Fly, RDS. The schema is applied
# automatically on first use (see src/lib/pg/migrations.ts), so a fresh database
# needs no manual setup.
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:password@host:5432/enginex
# Use sslmode=require for a managed provider.
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:password@host/enginex?sslmode=require
# DATABASE_POOL_MAX=10              # connections per instance. A serverless host
#                                   # (VERCEL=1) defaults to 1 instead, because
#                                   # every instance holds its own pool.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Identity — one workspace per consultancy, which is what makes this multi-tenant.
#
# With these set, each signed-in user is mapped to their own workspace through the
# members table: a first-time user gets one created and seeded, and no query in
# the app can reach another customer's rows because a tenant_id is a required
# argument on every call. See src/lib/tenant.ts.
#
# CLERK_SECRET_KEY=...
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=...
#
# Add your deployment's domain (and http://localhost:3403 while developing) to the
# instance's allowed origins in the Clerk dashboard, or the browser will refuse the
# session. Sign-in lives at /sign-in and sign-up at /sign-up; both are reachable
# without a session, and every other route sends a signed-out visitor there.
#
# A request that carries no session is refused, not served a guess — and that is
# correct: sign-in appearing to be on while everyone shares one workspace is worse
# than an error. To run without Clerk there are two deliberate ways out:
#
#   SINGLE_TENANT_SLUG  this deployment serves exactly one named workspace, in
#                       any environment. It is created and seeded on first use.
#                       Set it when one customer (or your own install) owns the
#                       deployment without sign-in. Every request is served that
#                       workspace — no per-user isolation — so the server logs a
#                       warning and you should not point a second customer at it.
#                       It outranks the CLERK_* variables above, so a deployment
#                       with idle Clerk keys keeps serving one customer.
# SINGLE_TENANT_SLUG=acme
#
#   DEV_TENANT_SLUG     the seeded development tenant, used when neither the
#                       identity provider nor SINGLE_TENANT_SLUG is configured
#                       and NODE_ENV is not production.
# DEV_TENANT_SLUG=dev

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Scheduler — protects the digest/alert endpoint.
# Set this before wiring a cron job: it is also what allows POST /api/cron to
# target a specific tenant by id.
# CRON_SECRET=...

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LLM — briefs, outreach drafts and the review agent.
# Recommended: the OpenLLM gateway (OpenAI-compatible). Create an account at
# https://openllm.sh, connect a provider, then set:
# OPENLLM_API_KEY=...
# OPENLLM_ENDPOINT=https://api.openllm.sh/v1
#
# Or talk to OpenAI directly:
# OPENAI_API_KEY=...
# OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://api.openai.com/v1
#
# ENGINEX_MODEL=gpt-4o-mini

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Signal sources — where accounts and signals actually come from.
#
# Each source is independent: set the ones you have and leave the rest blank.
# Unconfigured sources are skipped, and the /integrations page shows which ones
# are live. With nothing set, "Dry run" on that page still works against a
# bundled sample feed, and a scheduled run pulls nothing rather than inventing
# data. See the README for the response shape each source expects.
#
# Every source also declares WHICH KIND OF EVIDENCE it feeds — news, job_board,
# tender, people or funding — and a signal rule is only matched against items from
# the channels it reads. A rule reading tender evidence can be perfect and still
# never fire if the only source configured here is a news feed; /integrations
# lists any channel your rules read that no source feeds.
#
# Webz.io News Search — press coverage for funding rounds and leadership moves.
# WEBZ_API_TOKEN=...
# WEBZ_QUERIES=["company raises funding round data platform","company appoints chief data officer"]
# WEBZ_MAX_RESULTS=10            # 50 is the practical ceiling
# WEBZ_BASE_URL=https://api.webz.io

# Generic REST sources — any JSON API you have credentials for: job boards, ATS
# feeds, tender portals, people data. Declare as many as you need, one per kind
# of evidence, in one variable. Each entry feeds exactly one channel, which is
# what lets a tender rule and a hiring rule read different APIs at the same time.
# {page} in a URL is replaced per page as long as the entry sets maxPages.
# INGEST_HTTP_SOURCES=[
#   {"label":"Acme job board","url":"https://api.example.com/jobs?page={page}","channel":"job_board","maxPages":3,
#    "itemsPath":"data.items","fieldMap":{"companyName":"company.name","text":"description","url":"link"}},
#   {"label":"UK tender portal","url":"https://tenders.example.gov.uk/search","channel":"tender",
#    "tokenEnv":"TENDER_API_TOKEN","itemsPath":"data.notices",
#    "fieldMap":{"companyName":"buyer.name","text":"description","url":"notice_url"}},
#   {"label":"People provider","url":"https://people.example.com/v1/search","channel":"people",
#    "tokenEnv":"PEOPLE_API_KEY"}
# ]
# Per entry: label, url (required), channel (job_board by default), token or
# tokenEnv (a variable name, so a key lives in one place), method, itemsPath,
# fieldMap, maxPages, and an optional id. A missing token variable, an unknown
# channel name or unparseable JSON is reported on /integrations rather than
# silently skipped.
# TENDER_API_TOKEN=...

# The original single-endpoint variables still work as before, and can be used
# alongside the list. This one is a job board unless INGEST_HTTP_CHANNEL says
# otherwise:
# INGEST_HTTP_URL=https://api.example.com/jobs?company=acme&page={page}
# INGEST_HTTP_TOKEN=...
# INGEST_HTTP_LABEL=Acme job board
# INGEST_HTTP_CHANNEL=job_board                        # job_board (default), news, tender, people, funding
# INGEST_HTTP_ITEMS_PATH=data.items                    # default: finds items/results/data/jobs/...
# INGEST_HTTP_FIELD_MAP={"companyName":"company.name","text":"description","url":"link","postedAt":"created_at"}
# INGEST_HTTP_METHOD=GET
# INGEST_HTTP_MAX_PAGES=3

# Sources with no API of their own push candidates to POST /api/ingest instead.
# A pushed candidate may declare "channel": "tender" | "job_board" | "news" |
# "people" | "funding" so rules reading other channels cannot match it; with no
# channel declared the item is read by every rule. Set a shared secret to keep
# that endpoint closed:
# INGEST_SECRET=...
#
# With no INGEST_SECRET a push is refused, in every environment: an open push
# endpoint lets anyone who finds the URL put items into the feed, where they rank
# as real evidence. Pulls and dry runs from the /integrations page are unaffected
# — they carry no candidates.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Destructive endpoints.
#
# POST /api/reset drops the workspace and re-seeds the sample market. It is a demo
# tool, the UI never calls it, and it refuses unless this is set: without sign-in,
# an unauthenticated wipe on a public URL is a data-loss button. Present it as the
# x-reset-secret header, or Authorization: Bearer <value> (see src/lib/guards.ts).
# RESET_SECRET=...
#
# A local demo that wants no secrets at all opts in by name. It is ignored in
# production, so leaving it set on a host cannot open either endpoint.
# ALLOW_UNGUARDED_DEMO=1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Direct email sending (opt-in).
# Leave unset to keep sends simulated. EngineX does not send by default — the
# report keeps sending/deliverability with a specialised tool.
#
# EMAIL_PROVIDER=resend            # or postmark, sendgrid
# EMAIL_API_KEY=...
# EMAIL_FROM falls back to the sender address in the service profile.
# EMAIL_BASE_URL=https://api.resend.com
# POSTMARK_STREAM=outbound

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HubSpot — the one integration that is an API rather than a webhook.
# Unset: accounts are still planned and logged, nothing is written (dry run).
# Create the token in HubSpot → Settings → Integrations → Private Apps, then
# set it here and restart. Scopes: crm.objects.companies.write,
# crm.objects.deals.write (+ .read, which the pipeline check needs) and
# crm.objects.contacts.write. The pipeline and stage new deals open in are
# chosen per workspace on /integrations, not here — they are customer data.
# HUBSPOT_ACCESS_TOKEN=pat-na1-...
# HUBSPOT_DRY_RUN=1                # force planning/logging even with a live token
# HUBSPOT_BASE_URL=https://api.hubapi.com

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local-only: where the old SQLite build kept its file. No longer used, and not
# read by the app — kept only so an existing shell profile does not surprise you.
# ENGINEX_DATA_DIR=
